rs are active \u2014 83 and 325 early (steps 23, 26), 610 and 617 mid (25, 17), 642 (18), 743 and 826 (19, 20), 881 (24), 1034, 1641 and 991 late (21, 22, 27) \u2014 are all the same fixed core of ~30 nodes (58,138,247,383,393,402,424,429,613,686,721,794,861,879,895,928,944,959,970,1014,1032,1071,1136,1283,1291,1605,1713,1757,1761,1999), with only occasional one-off extras (276, 530, 807, 1478, 1512, 1681). Comparing \u00b15000 half-windows at any candidate change point therefore yields Jaccard \u2248 0.85\u20130.91, i.e. dissimilarity \u2248 0.09\u20130.15 (step_28), far under the 0.6 criterion. The only temporal structure is a baton-passing rotation of which group member emits (steps 05\u201316), not a switch of partner regime. Very low triangle counts (step_29) confirm a bipartite senders\u2192core-audience structure rather than a community reorganisation."}, "raw_final_response": "**Conclusion:** The group's contact set is stable throughout. Peak before/after dissimilarity \u2248 0.09\u20130.15 (30 of ~33 partners shared), far below the 0.6 threshold. What changes over time is which *group member* is active (a rotating baton among 59\u219283/325\u2192610/617\u2192642\u2192743/826\u2192881/991\u21921034/1641), while all of them draw from the same fixed ~30-node core audience.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_8155967f\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [59, 83, 325, 610, 617, 642, 743, 826, 881, 991, 1034, 1641],\n  \"window\": [56322, 86322],\n  \"confidence\": 0.87,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_15\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"st